# Docs are the source of truth

Before any substantive planning, design, or feature work, you MUST read the relevant doc(s):

| If the user asks about… | Read first |
|---|---|
| Product scope, schema, business rules, pricing, launch plan | `docs/MVP_SPEC.md` |
| App layer rules (Presentation / Application / Domain / Data / Core) | `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` |
| Open decisions, deferred items, blockers | `docs/FOLLOWUPS.md` |
| Where we are right now, what's broken, what's verified | `docs/STATE.md` |
| How chats are organized | `docs/AGENTS.md` |

If `docs/MVP_SPEC.md` and code disagree, **the spec wins** until the spec is updated.

## Deferred items go in a file, not in chat memory

When the user says *"keep this for later"*, *"add to to-do list"*, *"future improvement"*, or you identify something out of scope:

- **DO** append a new bullet to the appropriate section of `docs/FOLLOWUPS.md` with the right priority emoji (🔴 🟠 🟡 🟢 ⚪).
- **DO NOT** say "I'll keep it in our future to-do list" without writing the file.
- **DO** confirm the write to the user with the file path and new bullet.

## When you discover state changes

If something becomes verified-working or newly broken during a session, update `docs/STATE.md`. Don't let the doc drift behind reality.

## When the spec needs to change

If you propose a change to product scope, schema, or layer rules, edit `docs/MVP_SPEC.md` (or `ARCHITECTURE.md`) in the same turn. Never let agreed-upon decisions live only in chat history.
